{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf55jRerGWKBhEdaHCUtf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Why it stopped\n\nThe executor failed on its first round. It was not an eval failure, not budget exhaustion, and not a permission denial.\n\n**Initiative `managed`** → run `07cd181c` (supervised, still `active`) → task `T-20260914-chat-output` → attempt `3c6ec5cf`, state **`parked`**.\n\nThe terminal event is unambiguous:\n\n```\n5 RECIPE_ROUND_RESERVED  round 1, strategy diagnose-repair-verify, deadline 11:08:05Z\n6 RECIPE_ROUND_STARTED   round 1\n7 ATTEMPT_PARKED         code=RECIPE_STOPPED  message=\"EXECUTION_FAILED: exit status 1\"\n```\n\nThe routed adapter `chat-fixture` started at `10:38:07.836Z` and exited **15 milliseconds later** with empty output and `terminal_outcome: failed`. Its executable is a stub that answers a version probe and nothing else:\n\n```bash\nif [[ \"${1:-}\" == --version ]]; then echo chat-fixture/1; exit 0; fi\nexit 1\n```\n\nSo the adapter passed the capability probe (`managed_recipe_v1`, `persistent_round_budget`, `signed_timeout` all advertised as available) and was then dispatched as the only eligible candidate — but it cannot perform work. Round 1 produced no diff, no eval evidence, and no output. The recipe's `on_failure: park_with_context` did exactly what it should: park rather than burn a second round on an unchanged environment.\n\nThe task's evals never ran. `a.txt` and `b.txt` are both 0 bytes, so `eval_1` (`a.txt` == `completed`) and `eval_2` (9 bytes) would fail on content that was never written; `eval_3` (`b.txt` empty) is vacuously satisfied. Nothing was produced in the attempt worktree either.\n\n## Remaining budget\n\n| | value |\n|---|---|\n| Recipe rounds | **1 of 3 used → 2 remain** |\n| No-progress counter | 0 of 2 |\n| Signed deadline | `2026-09-15T11:08:05Z` — **~28 min left** (now `10:39Z`) |\n| Lease fence | token 1, expires `10:43:01Z` |\n| Accepted tasks | 0/1; unproven capabilities: 1 |\n\nBudget is **not** exhausted. The blocker is environmental, not budgetary — which is why resuming right now would just consume a second round the same way. The `budget_iterations: 15` in the task front matter is the task-level retry policy; the binding managed limit here is the recipe's `max_rounds: 3`.\n\n## What I did and did not do\n\nRead-only throughout: `taskspec status` for the task and initiative (both declare `mutation: none`), plus direct reads of the artifact JSON, and the mesh SQLite state via a scratch copy (since `mesh status` on existing runtime \"may restart its daemon\"). I ran no `mesh` command, did not reset the budget, did not authorize a successor, and did not resume.\n\nThe recorded blocker — an adapter that cannot execute — has to be resolved before any resume is meaningful. That resolution, and any resume, needs your explicit authorization."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1249,"cache_read_input_tokens":32053,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1249},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c4f0dfa7-b07e-4479-871c-0aa06475583c","uuid":"725ed912-f775-4624-b53c-15386ca3abc5","timestamp":"2026-09-15T10:40:03.485Z","request_id":"req_011Cf55jQyAwnwp5WVFtVrZm"}
